WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.480
Before I started playing electric guitar
people just titled my music as folk

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:15.040
music which I thought was bizarre
but now that I don't play as much

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.080
acoustic, people say it's
indie. (I used to have long

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:23.199
hair). I wanted to learn about the
independent music scene and the

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:27.119
communities where the genre comes from,
so I partnered with my friends over at

00:00:27.119 --> 00:00:31.119
INDIE ALASKA to show me one Alaskan
artist's story.

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:35.680
Quinn Christopherson is a young musician
born and raised in the Last Frontier.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.040
After making independent music in
Anchorage for years he rose to relative

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.840
stardom in 2019
when he won the NPR Tiny Desk Concert

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.559
contest.
So Quinn if I wanted to make an indie

00:00:46.559 --> 00:00:49.520
song, what exactly
would I do? What steps would I take to

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.559
make a complete indie song? If you wanted
to make an indie song,

00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:55.920
I would tell you to pick someone you
love

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.280
and that you want to write about and
write a song about them.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.120
Or a poem, start there. So question for
you Quinn, do you think we should

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.840
collaborate on a song together?
Yeah absolutely. Let's get it. Let's do it.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.680
I have a feeling that, you know, you're
just gonna bring something special

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.960
to the table.
okay thank you thank you. I'm gonna try

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.400
I'm gonna try my darndest.

00:01:18.479 --> 00:01:23.840
To bring it to the indie table! That is indie!
Right? While we work on that song

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:27.119
together, let's learn more about the
indie music scene.

00:01:27.119 --> 00:01:31.920
Before indie described a band's sound,
independent music was just that,

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.920
independent. Taking a page from punk
rockers before them in the 70's,

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:39.119
indie bands followed the do-it-yourself
ethos of self-reliance

00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:42.560
and operating outside the mainstream
music culture.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:46.320
Since indie describes a band's outlook
more than their musical style,

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:52.880
independent musicians can range from the
electronic soul style of Frank Ocean,

00:01:52.880 --> 00:02:00.200
to the lyrical and melancholy sounds of
The Smiths.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:10.080
And like most genres, sound and style is
greatly influenced

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.879
by location.

00:02:16.319 --> 00:02:19.599
To learn more about the history of the
music scene in Alaska

00:02:19.599 --> 00:02:23.120
we spoke with Kurt Riemann, the owner of
Surreal Studios,

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.720
the oldest recording studio in Anchorage.
Every piece of music in Alaska is

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.160
independent music.
There's no label supporting

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.800
anything in Alaska.
So the people that stay here are people

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:39.920
that are
very fiercely, independently Alaskanites

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:44.959
who take whatever it is they're working
on whether it's folk or punk or rock

00:02:44.959 --> 00:02:51.360
or electronic or whatever and
put their own independent spin on it. In

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.800
another 50 to 100 years it's going to be
seen as the folk music of the time.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.200
Quinn acknowledges that his sudden rise
to fame might be considered an anomaly

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.840
in the independent music industry.
I kind of like skipped a lot of steps.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:09.040
Person wins this contest and then
they're like

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:16.319
in this like world all of a sudden, which
is I think unique. Other indie musicians

00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:23.280
they make a record, do it theirself,
upload it and then maybe a record label

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:27.120
gets hold of it and it's like, oh, we, you
know, will like this, we'll release this.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.640
And then they kind of get their start
from there.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.360
Before, I was working a full-time
job

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:39.040
and my music was solely kind of like
this therapy thing

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:42.480
and my songs are kind of crushing in
that way. And I think

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.519
now that I've been able to give my full
self

00:03:45.519 --> 00:03:48.799
and like my work hours to my music I've
been able to

00:03:48.799 --> 00:03:52.480
like experience it and use it in in all
sorts of different

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.159
emotions that I didn't before so I
think it's definitely

00:03:56.159 --> 00:04:03.439
changed. (All the things you've done
for me, I hope I can do

00:04:03.439 --> 00:04:07.519
some for you). Songs with dark somber
lyrics

00:04:07.519 --> 00:04:11.519
are common all over mainstream music
right now. Just look at the popularity of

00:04:11.519 --> 00:04:16.239
musicians like Lana Del Rey
and Post Malone, and recently when Billie

00:04:16.239 --> 00:04:20.479
Eilish made history
at the 2020 Grammys by collecting six of

00:04:20.479 --> 00:04:23.919
the seven top awards
with her album that she recorded in her

00:04:23.919 --> 00:04:29.440
brother's bedroom in their family home.
Clearly, people are drawn to this style.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:34.160
In the indie music scene
a DIY style of recording music is common.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:38.400
Quinn told us he once recorded a song by
putting a sock over a microphone.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.800
Indie music also tends to be more
sonically adventurous or experimental.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:46.960
Some independent bands like Neutral Milk Hotel sound purposefully raw

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:57.840
with pitchy vocals and over modulated
instrumental sections.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:01.680
Hey Quinn tell me about your
time when you

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:05.440
entered the Tiny Desk Competition and
you won. What was that like?

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:09.199
Best thing that's ever happened to me for sure.
It was like

00:05:09.199 --> 00:05:13.520
seriously amazing. We entered last year
and I say we cause it's like a community

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:23.919
affair, like making a video and
putting it together and putting it out.

00:05:23.919 --> 00:05:28.720
(I'm tired of people trying to
erase me)

00:05:31.919 --> 00:05:36.240
It was just crazy. I'm just gonna tune up
this guitar.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:40.880
Looking at you Bob, because
I don't know if you know this but when

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:46.479
you called me and you told me
you won I got off the phone and I

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:50.960
thought, dang I should buy a guitar.

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:54.479
Legit did not have one.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:00.000
But that's Anchorage. That's the music
community there. Everyone just borrowed

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.120
me their stuff
long term. It was

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:11.199
unreal. Everybody hot has to hit

00:06:11.199 --> 00:06:14.479
Tiny Desk.
Oh my god. Tiny Desk is like the

00:06:14.479 --> 00:06:18.720
stage.
So Quinn what inspires the themes that

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:23.440
you
put into your music. Life experiences.

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:26.800
I feel like i'm a storyteller (Storytelling!).

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:32.800
Yeah I like telling stories and
pulling from like people I love and

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:36.880
people I know and
and that sort of thing. So quinn should

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:40.240
we check out this song? Should we see how
it's going?

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.840
Let's listen to it. But I didn't
have a mic, I didn't have an overhead

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:46.880
so it's gonna be like a real washy like
a weirdly miced

00:06:46.880 --> 00:07:05.549
indie kind of thing. Okay that's perfect!
Let's get into it.

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:23.649
Yeah, that's way more vibrant already.
I play with the bass though.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:33.560
(He got me my own ball and matching shoes)

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:46.640
(I was a real bad sport. I never liked to lose)

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:54.020
(He kept showing up teaching me the rules)

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:07.580
(Oh my dad. Yeah he plays guitar. Says he knows more than he can do)

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:15.440
(He tells me that I do more than I know)

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:35.580
(He'd tell me don't be stupid when I hang with friends. And look me in the eye. My dad has always been a real smart guy)

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:41.440
I'm telling you when I recorded the
vocals I recorded them in my backyard

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:47.120
that's why all the birds and everything.
Oh! That stuff was real?! Yes! Oh I thought that was like...man.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:52.640
No, I'm telling you and you hear in the
beginning of the song, "Kalia! Kalia!"

00:08:53.279 --> 00:08:56.480
That's my neighbors' kids I think they're
just like playing.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:00.560
And see that's the that's really the
the essence, the organic

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:04.880
essence. Yeah so the fact that you did
that on your end too is hilarious.

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:08.800
Do you think living in Alaska influences
your music at all?

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:15.600
Yeah I think the weather influences me
in general. It's just like so freaking dark

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:19.519
for like eight months,
you know? Yeah yeah yeah yeah. I think

00:09:19.519 --> 00:09:24.240
that affects my writing for sure.
Despite indie music's current dark

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:28.160
streak, many artists these days
are open about their own mental health

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:33.760
and shed light on things like depression
and anxiety. I've been therapized

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:37.920
at a few shows. When I wrote those songs,
I was

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:43.760
playing for nobody. That was true therapy.
Now, pouring my heart out in front of

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:47.200
like strangers
and them just really willing to take me

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:49.680
in is so crazy. I
never thought that would happen in a

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:52.560
million years. So it's like, I'm really
grateful.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:10:00.640
 

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:04.640
Quinn is currently working on his first
full-length album.

